<Layout
  title="Privacy — Some Kaizen"
  description="Some Kaizen collects nothing, because it has nowhere to send it. What that means precisely, and what would change if sync is ever built."
>
  <Prose
    title="Privacy"
    standfirst="The app collects nothing. Not as a policy anyone has to keep — as a fact about how it is built."
    updated="18 August 2026"
  >
    <h2>The application</h2>
    <p>
      Some Kaizen has no account system, no server, and makes no network requests. Every habit,
      entry, plan, block of time and preference is written to your device's own storage
      (IndexedDB inside the app's WebView) and read back from there. There is nowhere for it to
      be sent, which is a stronger guarantee than a promise not to send it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>No analytics, telemetry, crash reporting or attribution.</li>
      <li>No advertising and no advertising identifiers.</li>
      <li>No third-party SDKs that phone home.</li>
      <li>No sign-in, no email address, no profile.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The app asks for exactly one permission, and only at the moment it is needed: permission
      to post a notification, asked the first time you set a reminder. It never asks for storage
      access — exporting writes into the app's own directory and hands the file to the system
      share sheet, and importing uses the system document picker.
    </p>

    <h2>Your data leaving is something you do</h2>
    <p>
      Backups and shared routines are files. You choose to write one and you choose where it
      goes. A shared routine deliberately carries names, durations and an hour and nothing else
      — no identifiers, no dates, no history — so a routine handed to somebody cannot address
      anything already on their device.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site is static files. It sets no cookies, uses no analytics, and loads nothing from
      any other host: the typeface is served from this domain, and every screenshot is a local
      image. The tools hold their state in your browser and send nothing anywhere. Your host or
      CDN may keep ordinary server access logs; nothing on the page adds to them.
    </p>

    <h2>If sync is ever built</h2>
    <p>
      Sync between devices and hosted backup are planned, and they would be paid. A service
      that stores data does handle data, so that is not a thing to slip in quietly under this
      page. It would arrive as something you opt into, with its own terms written before it
      ships, and the offline app would continue to work exactly as it does now for anybody who
      never touches it. Nothing described above changes until then.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      The app collects nothing from anyone, of any age. It is not directed at children and has
      no social features, messaging, or purchases.
    </p>

    <h2>Checking rather than believing</h2>
    <p>
      Everything on this page is checkable. The source is public at
      <a href="https://github.com/yebt/some-kaizen" rel="noopener">github.com/yebt/some-kaizen</a>,
      and a privacy claim you can read the code behind is the only kind worth making.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
